title: SolarWinds 2020
page: breaches/solarwinds.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Russian SVR, per the US and UK governments, put SUNBURST into signed Orion updates that up to 18,000 customers downloaded, by SolarWinds' count, then forged Golden SAML tokens at high-value government targets to reach Azure AD and Microsoft 365.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 18,000 | customers, at most, downloaded affected Orion versions | up to 18,000, by SolarWinds' count
stat: ~100 | private sector companies, plus 9 federal agencies, compromised | per the White House, February 2021
stat: ~14 | months from the first tampered Orion build to public disclosure | October 2019 to December 2020
The page names the actor as APT29, or Cozy Bear: Russia's SVR, per the US and UK governments in April 2021.
notes:
Start with the page's headline figures, and with who stands behind each one.

Up to 18,000 is SolarWinds' own count of customers who downloaded affected Orion versions. The page says that early figure included customers who never installed them, and customers who installed them on servers without internet access. Keep up to, and SolarWinds' name, with the number.

About 100 private sector companies and 9 federal agencies were compromised, the White House said in February 2021, without naming them. SolarWinds gives its own, separate estimate on a later slide. Keep the two apart.

About 14 months passed from the first tampered Orion build, in October 2019, to public disclosure in December 2020. When the attackers first got in is not known, and SolarWinds says they had access at least nine months before that build.

The page names the threat actor as APT29, also called Cozy Bear. The US and UK governments attributed the campaign to the SVR, Russia's Foreign Intelligence Service, in April 2021. APT stands for advanced persistent threat.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: Not known | Attackers get in, at least nine months before the test run, per SolarWinds
event: October 2019 | First tampered Orion build; SolarWinds calls it a test run
event: About March 2020 | First SUNBURST updates, per Unit 42; customers install them
event: Date not given | At selected targets: domain admin, then forged SAML into Azure AD and M365
event: December 13, 2020 | FireEye's public disclosure triggers CISA's Emergency Directive 21-01
About 14 months from the first tampered build to public disclosure. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The timeline shows only the dates the page gives, with their sources.

When the attackers first got in is not known. SolarWinds says they had persistent access to its software development environment and internal systems for at least nine months before the test run.

In October 2019, per SolarWinds, the attackers tested injecting code into Orion builds. Unit 42 found modified Orion code as early as October 2019, but says the first weaponized updates, SUNBURST, were not released until approximately March 2020. From March 2020, customers installed the trojanized updates, and SUNBURST beaconed over DNS.

At selected high-value targets, the attackers gained domain admin on-premises, stole the ADFS token-signing certificate and forged SAML tokens to reach Azure AD and Microsoft 365. The page gives no date for these steps, and its summary says the access lasted for months.

On December 13, 2020, FireEye's public disclosure triggered a global incident response and CISA Emergency Directive 21-01. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency. In April 2021, the US and UK governments attributed the campaign to the SVR.

The markers are evenly spaced, so do not read the gaps as durations.

== Downloaded is not the same as hacked
layout: compare
col: Up to 18,000 downloaded, per SolarWinds | Customers who downloaded affected Orion versions | Includes customers who never installed them | And some who installed them on servers without internet access
col: Fewer than 100 hacked, per SolarWinds | Customers hacked through SUNBURST | SolarWinds' estimate, May 2021
Both figures are SolarWinds' own. The page gives the White House's count separately.
notes:
Slow down here. Downloading the update and being hacked through it are very different numbers, and both of these come from SolarWinds.

SolarWinds' early figure of up to 18,000 customers counted downloads of the affected versions. It includes customers who never installed them, or installed them on servers without internet access.

In May 2021, SolarWinds estimated that the actual number of customers hacked through SUNBURST was fewer than 100. The page also says SVR selectively activated only high-value targets for further exploitation.

The White House figure on the first slide, about 100 private sector companies and 9 federal agencies, is a separate count from a different source. Do not set it against SolarWinds' estimate or add the two together; the page does neither. When you quote any of these numbers, say whose it is.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Supply Chain: Build System Compromise | SUNSPOT in the build | T1195.002
step: 02 | Backdoor Activation and C2 Beaconing | Beacons over DNS | T1071.004
step: 03 | On-Prem Privilege Escalation | Domain admin on-prem | T1078.002
step: 04 | On-Prem to Cloud Pivot: Golden SAML | ADFS signing key stolen | T1606.002
step: 05 | Cloud Espionage and Persistence | M365 mail, backdoors | T1114.002
step: 06 | Discovery | FireEye traces Orion | -
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and its first-listed ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is the build system compromise, where SUNSPOT put SUNBURST into Orion. Stage 02 is the backdoor's activation and its beaconing over DNS. C2 means command and control, the channel an attacker uses to direct malware. Stage 03 is the on-premises step: TEARDROP deployed Cobalt Strike BEACON, and SVR obtained domain admin. Stage 04 is the pivot to the cloud: the ADFS token-signing key stolen and Golden SAML tokens forged. Stage 05 is long-term email access in Microsoft 365 and backdoors in Azure AD. Stage 06 is discovery, which the page maps to no technique, so its box shows none.

Read the arrows as the order of stages. The page says SVR selectively activated only high-value targets for further exploitation. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== SUNSPOT put SUNBURST into Orion's build
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Supply Chain: Build System Compromise
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Implant | SUNSPOT, watching MSBuild.exe, per CrowdStrike and FireEye
fact: Test run | October 2019, per SolarWinds; access began earlier, date unknown
fact: Dormancy | about two weeks; a pause that helped cover their tracks, per CyberArk
Signed with the legitimate certificate, the trojanized DLL appeared authentic.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the attack entered the software itself. The page maps it to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

SVR gained access to SolarWinds' internal build system and installed SUNSPOT, a build-time implant. Per CrowdStrike and FireEye, it monitored the MSBuild.exe process, which is Microsoft's build engine, and injected SUNBURST's malicious code into SolarWinds.Orion.Core.BusinessLayer.dll during compilation. The resulting DLL was then signed with SolarWinds' legitimate code-signing certificate, which made it appear authentic.

Keep the dates straight. When the attackers first got in is not known. SolarWinds says they had persistent access for at least nine months before a test run in October 2019, when they tested injecting code into Orion builds. Unit 42 says the first weaponized updates, SUNBURST, were not released until approximately March 2020.

Once installed, SUNBURST stayed dormant for about two weeks, which CyberArk calls an intentional pause that helped the attacker cover their tracks.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what a code signature proves.

== What a code signature proves, and what it does not
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Introduction to Code Signing | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/internet-explorer/ie-developer/platform-apis/ms537361(v=vs.85)
credit: Also from Authenticode digital signatures (learn.microsoft.com)
Code signing addresses authenticity, telling users where code came from, and integrity, showing it has not been tampered with since its publication.

Microsoft adds that Authenticode itself cannot guarantee that signed code is safe to run.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains code signing in general, from Microsoft's documentation, and says nothing about the incident.

Microsoft names two issues to address for software distributed over the internet. Authenticity assures users where the code came from. Integrity verifies that the code has not been tampered with since its publication.

Authenticode is Microsoft's code-signing technology. It identifies the publisher of signed software and verifies that the software has not changed since it was signed and published. The publisher's certificate is issued by a certification authority after it verifies the publisher's identity, and it chains up to a trusted root.

The signature itself works on a hash. A one-way hash of the code is signed with the publisher's private key; the recipient hashes the code again and checks the signed hash with the publisher's public key. If the two match, the signature is valid and the code is intact.

Microsoft is careful about the limit: Authenticode itself cannot guarantee that signed code is safe to run.

Also from Authenticode digital signatures: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/install/authenticode

== From March 2020, SUNBURST beaconed over DNS
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Backdoor Activation and C2 Beaconing
technique: T1071.004 | Application Layer Protocol: DNS
fact: Delivery | trojanized Orion updates, installed by customers
fact: C2 domain | avsvmcloud[.]com, attacker-controlled
fact: Encoding | victim environment information in DNS subdomain queries
fact: Evasion | posing as the Orion Improvement Program protocol, per FireEye
SVR selectively activated only high-value targets for further exploitation.
notes:
From March 2020, customers installed the trojanized Orion updates. The page maps this stage to T1071.004, Application Layer Protocol: DNS.

SUNBURST beaconed to the attacker-controlled domain avsvmcloud[.]com using DNS subdomain queries that encoded information about the victim's environment. DNS is the Domain Name System, which turns names into network addresses. A beacon is a periodic check-in from malware to its controller.

The traffic mimicked legitimate SolarWinds telemetry, masquerading as the Orion Improvement Program protocol, per FireEye. CyberArk says the dormancy of about two weeks, from stage 01, helped the attacker cover their tracks.

SVR then selectively activated only high-value targets for further exploitation. In May 2021, SolarWinds estimated that fewer than 100 customers were hacked through SUNBURST, against its early figure of up to 18,000 downloads.

The page ties no defense to this stage. That gap comes back in the discussion at the end.

The next slide explains, in general terms, how DNS can carry command and control traffic.

== How DNS can carry command and control traffic
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1071.004 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/004/
ATT&CK says adversaries may use DNS for command and control to blend in with existing traffic.

DNS is common, may be allowed even before network authentication completes, and its packets have many fields where data can be concealed.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System application layer protocol to avoid detection or network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often their results, are embedded in the traffic between client and server.

DNS serves an administrative function in networking, so it may be very common in an environment, and it may be allowed even before network authentication is completed. DNS packets contain many fields and headers in which data can be concealed. ATT&CK says abusing DNS to communicate with systems an adversary controls, while mimicking normal traffic, is often known as DNS tunneling.

It also describes DNS beaconing, where commands are sent through DNS queries and may be embedded in different record types, such as TXT or A records. ATT&CK says DNS beacons may be difficult to detect because they communicate infrequently, which hides the malicious traffic among normal DNS traffic. The page does not say how SUNBURST received commands.

== At selected targets, TEARDROP deployed BEACON
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: On-Prem Privilege Escalation
technique: T1078.002 | Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts
fact: Dropper | TEARDROP, which FireEye calls a memory-only dropper
fact: BEACON | Cobalt Strike, for interactive C2 and lateral movement
fact: BEACON C2 | mobilnweb[.]com, a separate domain, in Unit 42's sample
fact: Goal | reach ADFS and steal the SAML token-signing certificate
The sources do not say exactly how SVR reached domain admin on-premises.
notes:
At selected high-value targets, SUNBURST delivered TEARDROP, which FireEye calls a memory-only dropper. A dropper is malware whose job is to install other malware. TEARDROP deployed Cobalt Strike BEACON for interactive command and control and for lateral movement, which means moving from one system to others in the same network.

The BEACON sample Unit 42 analyzed contains a beacon request to a separate domain, mobilnweb[.]com, with a Chrome browser User-Agent. The page says the SolarWinds-mimicking traffic was SUNBURST's, at stage 02.

Domain admin privileges were obtained on the victim's on-premises Active Directory, and the page maps this stage to T1078.002, Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts. The sources do not say exactly how; the next slide gives what CyberArk and MITRE say. With that access, SVR was positioned to attack cloud identity via the ADFS server, whose SAML token-signing certificate was the goal. ADFS is Active Directory Federation Services, Microsoft's on-premises service that signs users in to other services.

The page ties no defense to the TEARDROP, BEACON or domain admin steps; the discussion at the end returns to that.

== What CyberArk and MITRE say near this step
layout: compare
col: CyberArk | Most likely harvested credentials stored in Orion's database | Including Active Directory credentials | Which would enable rapid escalation of privileges
col: MITRE C0024 | APT29 used domain administrators' accounts | To help facilitate lateral movement
The page says its sources do not state exactly how SVR reached domain admin.
notes:
The page is careful about this step, and you should be too.

The sources do not say exactly how SVR reached domain admin on the victim's on-premises Active Directory. CyberArk says the actor most likely harvested credentials stored in Orion's database, including Active Directory credentials, which would enable rapid escalation of privileges. Keep CyberArk's words most likely when you say this aloud.

MITRE's entry for the campaign, C0024, says APT29 used domain administrators' accounts to help facilitate lateral movement.

With that access, the page says, SVR was positioned to attack cloud identity via the ADFS server.

The next slide explains Cobalt Strike in general terms.

== What Cobalt Strike is
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK, Cobalt Strike software entry | https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154/
ATT&CK describes Cobalt Strike as a commercial, full-featured remote access tool that bills itself as adversary simulation software.

Its interactive post-exploit capabilities cover the full range of ATT&CK tactics, in one integrated system.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK's software entry, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Cobalt Strike as a commercial, full-featured, remote access tool. It bills itself as adversary simulation software designed to execute targeted attacks and emulate the post-exploitation actions of advanced threat actors.

ATT&CK says Cobalt Strike's interactive post-exploit capabilities cover the full range of ATT&CK tactics, all executed within a single, integrated system. Post-exploitation means what an operator does after first gaining access to a system.

== Golden SAML: tokens that can claim any user
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: On-Prem to Cloud Pivot: Golden SAML
technique: T1606.002 | Forge Web Credentials: SAML Tokens
technique: T1550 | Use Alternate Authentication Material
fact: Taken | ADFS token-signing key and certificate, with domain admin
fact: Can forge | SAML assertions impersonating any user
fact: Accepted by | Azure AD and M365, as fully legitimate
fact: Persistence | could last lengthy periods; certs almost never changed (CyberArk)
Forged SAML bypasses MFA entirely: the assertion is the proof of authentication.
notes:
With domain admin privileges, SVR extracted the ADFS token-signing private key and certificate from the on-premises federation server. Using that key, it could forge SAML assertions impersonating any user. The page calls this Golden SAML, and maps it to T1606.002, Forge Web Credentials: SAML Tokens, and T1550, Use Alternate Authentication Material.

SAML, the Security Assertion Markup Language, is a standard for one system to vouch for a user's sign-in to another. A SAML assertion is that signed statement.

The page explains why MFA did not help. Forged SAML tokens bypass MFA entirely because the SAML assertion is the proof of authentication, and no second factor is requested when a valid SAML response is presented. MFA is multi-factor authentication.

On persistence, the page quotes CyberArk: SAML token-signing certificates are almost never changed, so the attacker could persist in the network for lengthy periods of time without fear of detection. Keep CyberArk's could.

The next slide lists the attack steps as the page gives them.

== Golden SAML in four steps, as the page lists them
layout: table
row: Step | What the attacker does
row: 1 | Extract the ADFS private signing key and certificate, which requires domain admin
row: 2 | Forge a SAML assertion claiming to be any privileged user, such as Global Admin
row: 3 | Present it to Azure AD or M365, which accept it as fully legitimate
row: 4 | MFA is bypassed, because the forged SAML is the authentication proof
No second factor is requested when a valid SAML response is presented.
notes:
These are the page's own four steps for a Golden SAML attack.

First, extract the ADFS private signing key and certificate. The page notes this requires domain admin, which is why stage 03 mattered.

Second, forge a SAML assertion claiming to be any privileged user, such as a Global Admin, the highest-privilege administrator role in Azure AD.

Third, present the assertion to Azure AD or Microsoft 365, where it is accepted as fully legitimate.

Fourth, MFA is bypassed, because the forged SAML is itself the authentication proof. No second factor is requested when a valid SAML response is presented.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what a token-signing certificate does in AD FS.

== What an AD FS token-signing certificate does
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Token-Signing Certificates | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-fs/design/token-signing-certificates
A federation server signs each security token with the private key of its token-signing certificate.

A partner checks the signature with the public key, confirming that a valid federation server issued the token and that it was not modified.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft's AD FS documentation, in general terms.

Microsoft says federation servers require token-signing certificates to prevent attackers from altering or counterfeiting security tokens in an attempt to gain unauthorized access to federated resources.

Every token-signing certificate has a private key and a public key. The federation server digitally signs a security token with the private key. A partner that receives the token uses the public key to verify that it was in fact issued by that federation server and was not modified in transit.

Microsoft calls this key pairing the most important validation mechanism of any federated partnership. For a token-signing certificate to sign a security token at all, it must contain a private key, and the AD FS service account must have access to that private key.

== M365 mail access, and Azure AD backdoors
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Cloud Espionage and Persistence
technique: T1114.002 | Email Collection: Remote Email Collection
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: Email | M365 environments at multiple US government agencies
fact: Federation | new trusted identity providers added to Azure AD
fact: OAuth | app permissions granted for API-based access
fact: Service principals | credentials added for ongoing access
These backdoors persisted even after Orion was removed from victim networks.
notes:
SVR accessed Microsoft 365 environments at multiple US government agencies. The page maps the email access to T1114.002, Remote Email Collection. The next slide gives the victims the page names, each with its source.

SVR also modified Azure AD. It added new trusted federated identity providers, granted OAuth application permissions for API-based access, and added service principal credentials for ongoing access. The page maps these changes to T1098, Account Manipulation. OAuth is a standard that lets an application act with granted permissions instead of a user's password. A service principal is an application's identity in a tenant.

The page calls the lesson a key one. These cloud-layer backdoors persisted even after SolarWinds Orion was removed from victim networks, so Azure AD had to be separately evicted.

== Stage 05's victims, and who said so
layout: table
row: Source | What it said
row: Justice Department | The activity involved access to its Microsoft O365 email environment
row: Senator Ron Wyden | Microsoft notified Treasury that dozens of email accounts were compromised
row: White House, February 2021 | 9 federal agencies and about 100 private sector companies compromised, not named
SVR accessed M365 environments at multiple US government agencies, the page says. These are separate statements; the page does not combine them.
notes:
The page names two agencies, each on a named source's word, and gives the White House's count separately.

The Justice Department said the activity involved access to the Department's Microsoft O365 email environment. O365 is Office 365, the earlier name for Microsoft 365.

Senator Ron Wyden said Microsoft had notified the Treasury Department that dozens of email accounts were compromised.

The White House said in February 2021 that 9 federal agencies and about 100 private sector companies were compromised, without naming them.

Say each of these with its source. The page does not combine them into one count.

The next slide explains service principals in general terms.

== What a service principal is in Microsoft Entra ID
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Application and service principal objects in Microsoft Entra ID | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals
credit: Also from New name for Azure Active Directory (learn.microsoft.com)
Microsoft Entra ID is the new name for Azure AD. An application reaches a tenant's resources through a service principal.

It defines what the app can do in that tenant, who can access it, and what it can access.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft renamed Azure Active Directory, or Azure AD, to Microsoft Entra ID. Existing deployments and configurations kept working under the new name.

To access resources secured by a Microsoft Entra tenant, an entity must be represented by a security principal: a user principal for a person, and a service principal for an application. The security principal defines the access policy and permissions for that user or application in the tenant.

When an application is registered, it gets an application object, a template that lives in its home tenant. A service principal is created in every tenant where the application is used, including when the application is given permission through consent. The service principal object defines what the app can do in that tenant, who can access the app, and what resources it can access.

An app registration can also hold secrets or certificates, the credentials the app uses to work.

Also from New name for Azure Active Directory: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/new-name

== FireEye traced the theft of its tools to Orion
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Discovery
fact: Found by | FireEye, investigating its own breach
fact: Disclosed | December 13, 2020
fact: To disclosure | ~14 months from the first tampered Orion build, October 2019
fact: CISA ED 21-01 | federal civilian agencies ordered to disconnect or power down affected Orion
fact: After Orion removal | Azure AD federation and OAuth app backdoors persisted
Removing Orion did not remove cloud persistence; Azure AD needed separate eviction.
notes:
FireEye discovered the theft of its proprietary red team offensive tools during an internal investigation, and traced the intrusion to a trojanized SolarWinds Orion update. A red team is a group that attacks its own organization, or a client's, to test its defenses. The page maps this stage to no ATT&CK technique.

FireEye's public disclosure on December 13, 2020 triggered a global incident response and CISA Emergency Directive 21-01, ED 21-01 on the slide. It required federal civilian agencies running affected Orion versions to immediately disconnect or power them down. The page puts about 14 months between the first tampered Orion build, in October 2019, and disclosure, and SolarWinds says the attackers had access at least nine months before that build.

Then the complication the page stresses. Removing Orion did not remove cloud persistence. The cloud-layer backdoors in Azure AD federation and OAuth apps persisted after Orion was removed, and required a separate, comprehensive eviction process.

The next slides draw that pattern out.

== From a build system to cloud identity, as the page describes it
layout: layers
layer: SolarWinds | SUNSPOT injects SUNBURST into Orion's build
layer: Customers | SUNBURST; selected targets: TEARDROP, BEACON, domain admin
layer: ADFS server | Token-signing key and certificate stolen
layer: Cloud identity | Azure AD and M365 accept forged SAML; backdoors added
CISA says the actors moved laterally from the enterprise networks they had entered to Microsoft cloud systems, Azure AD and Microsoft 365.
notes:
This slide draws the path the page describes, from one vendor's build system into its customers' cloud identity. Each arrow is a step the page states.

SUNSPOT put SUNBURST into Orion during SolarWinds' build, and customers installed the trojanized updates. At selected high-value targets, SUNBURST delivered TEARDROP, which deployed Cobalt Strike BEACON, and SVR obtained domain admin; the page says the sources do not say exactly how. With domain admin, SVR stole the ADFS token-signing key and certificate. Forged SAML tokens were then accepted by Azure AD and Microsoft 365, and SVR added backdoors in Azure AD.

CISA says the actors moved laterally from the enterprise networks they had entered to Microsoft cloud systems, Azure AD and Microsoft 365. That move to the cloud is why the next slide matters.

== Disconnecting Orion left the cloud backdoors in place
layout: compare
col: Disconnecting or powering down Orion | ED 21-01 ordered it for federal civilian agencies running affected versions | Removing Orion did not remove cloud access
col: Cloud persistence added in Azure AD | New federated identity providers | OAuth app permissions for API-based access | Service principal credentials for ongoing access
Azure AD had to be separately evicted, the page says.
notes:
This is the lesson the page returns to at stages 05 and 06.

When FireEye's disclosure triggered CISA Emergency Directive 21-01, federal civilian agencies running affected Orion versions were ordered to immediately disconnect or power them down. Removing Orion did not remove cloud persistence.

At stage 05, SVR added new federated identity providers to Azure AD, granted OAuth app permissions for API-based access, and added service principal credentials for ongoing access. The page says these cloud-layer backdoors persisted even after Orion was removed from victim networks.

So Azure AD had to be separately evicted, in what the page calls a separate, comprehensive eviction process. Keep that sequence in mind for the defenses.

== Five defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: ADFS as a Tier 0 asset | 04
row: Correlate ADFS and Azure AD sign-in logs | 04
row: Audit federated IdPs, OAuth apps, service principals | 05
row: Supply chain integrity for build systems | 01
row: Cloud eviction as a separate step | 05 | 06
No defense on the page names stage 02's DNS beaconing, or TEARDROP, BEACON or domain admin at stage 03.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's five defenses against its six stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something from that stage.

Treating ADFS as a Tier 0 asset protects the signing keys taken at stage 04. Correlating ADFS and Azure AD sign-in logs detects Golden SAML, also stage 04. Auditing federated identity providers, OAuth app permissions and service principals names the three backdoors added at stage 05. Supply chain integrity for build systems names the build environment of stage 01. Planning cloud eviction as a separate step names stage 05's backdoors and the separate eviction stage 06 describes.

Now the empty columns. No defense names stage 02's DNS beaconing, or TEARDROP, BEACON or the domain admin escalation at stage 03. Stage 03 names the ADFS server as the goal of its on-premises access, and the Tier 0 defense protects that server. The theft it guards against, of the signing keys, happened at stage 04, so the matrix marks 04 and leaves 03 empty. Weigh that for yourself in the discussion at the end.

== Protect ADFS as Tier 0, and watch for Golden SAML
layout: compare
col: ADFS as Tier 0 (stage 04) | The ADFS server holds the keys to all federated services | Privileged access workstations and no internet exposure | HSM-protected signing keys; monitor it like your most critical system
col: Detect Golden SAML (stage 04) | Correlate ADFS event logs with Azure AD sign-in logs | Legitimate ADFS authentications leave traces in both | An Azure AD sign-in with no ADFS event (IDs 1202, 1200) is highly suspicious
Treat your ADFS or identity provider as a Tier 0 asset, equal to domain controllers.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 04.

First, treat your ADFS or identity provider as a Tier 0 asset, equal to domain controllers. Tier 0 means the systems that control identity for everything else. The page says the ADFS server holds the keys to all federated services. Protect it with privileged access workstations, which are hardened machines used only for administration, keep it off the internet, and keep its signing keys in an HSM, a hardware security module that holds keys so they cannot simply be copied out. Monitor it as you would your most critical production system.

Second, detect Golden SAML by correlating ADFS event logs with Azure AD sign-in logs. Legitimate ADFS authentications leave traces in both systems. An Azure AD sign-in with no corresponding ADFS authentication event, IDs 1202 or 1200, is highly suspicious. Keep the page's wording, highly suspicious, when you say it aloud.

== Audit Azure AD, and plan cloud eviction as its own step
layout: compare
col: Audit regularly (stage 05) | Federated IdPs, OAuth app permissions, service principals | Entra audit logs or Defender for Cloud Apps | Detect new high-privilege applications
col: Evict separately (stages 05 and 06) | Any on-prem compromise may have resulted in cloud backdoors | Review and evict Azure AD, OAuth permissions, federated IdPs | A step separate from on-prem remediation
SVR's cloud persistence survived Orion removal, the page says.
notes:
Two defenses deal with what SVR left in the cloud.

First, audit Azure AD federated identity providers, OAuth app permissions and service principals regularly, because SVR's cloud persistence survived Orion removal. The page names two tools: Microsoft Entra audit logs, or Defender for Cloud Apps, to detect new high-privilege applications. An IdP is an identity provider, the system that signs users in.

Second, plan for cloud-specific eviction as a separate step from on-prem remediation. The page says any on-prem compromise may have resulted in cloud-layer backdoors. Azure AD, OAuth app permissions and federated identity providers must be independently reviewed and evicted. Keep the word may when you say this aloud.

== Treat the build pipeline as production infrastructure
layout: text
The page calls for software supply chain integrity verification for build systems.
- Monitor build environments with endpoint security.
- Verify build artifact integrity, and implement reproducible builds.
- Treat your CI/CD pipeline as production infrastructure.
notes:
This defense answers stage 01, where SUNSPOT sat inside SolarWinds' build system and injected SUNBURST during compilation.

The page calls for software supply chain integrity verification for build systems. Monitor build environments with endpoint security. Verify build artifact integrity. Implement reproducible builds, meaning builds that produce the same output from the same source. And treat your CI/CD pipeline as production infrastructure. CI/CD is continuous integration and continuous delivery, the automated pipeline that builds, tests and ships code.

Recall stage 01: SUNSPOT ran inside the build, and the trojanized DLL was then signed with SolarWinds' legitimate certificate, which made it appear authentic.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Is your ADFS server, or other identity provider, protected as a Tier 0 asset?
check: Are its signing keys HSM-protected, and is it kept off the internet?
check: Would an Azure AD sign-in with no matching ADFS event raise an alert?
check: When did you last audit federated IdPs, OAuth app permissions and service principals?
check: Would you notice a new high-privilege application in your audit logs?
check: Are your build environments monitored, with artifact integrity verified?
check: Does your incident plan treat cloud eviction as a step separate from on-prem remediation?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your ADFS server, or whatever identity provider you federate through, is protected as a Tier 0 asset: privileged access workstations, no internet exposure, and HSM-protected signing keys.

Ask whether anything would alert on an Azure AD sign-in with no corresponding ADFS authentication event, IDs 1202 or 1200.

Ask when federated identity providers, OAuth app permissions and service principals were last audited, and whether Microsoft Entra audit logs or Defender for Cloud Apps would show a new high-privilege application.

Ask whether your build environments run endpoint security, and whether you verify build artifact integrity.

Finally, ask whether your incident plan treats cloud eviction as its own step, separate from on-prem remediation.

== After on-premises cleanup, what could remain in your cloud?
layout: question
At stage 05, SVR added federated identity providers, OAuth app permissions and service principal credentials in Azure AD. Removing Orion did not remove cloud access, the page says.

Your on-premises incident is closed. What would you check in your cloud?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

At stage 05, SVR added new federated identity providers to Azure AD, granted OAuth app permissions and added service principal credentials. After CISA Emergency Directive 21-01 ordered federal civilian agencies to disconnect or power down affected Orion versions, the page says, the cloud-layer backdoors persisted after Orion was removed.

Ask each person to imagine their own on-premises incident has just been closed. What in their cloud tenant would they look at, with which logs, and who would own the work? The answer slide gives what the page says.

== What the page says about cloud eviction
layout: answer
Plan cloud-specific eviction as a separate step from on-prem remediation.
- Independently review and evict Azure AD, OAuth app permissions and federated IdPs.
- Audit federated IdPs, OAuth app permissions and service principals regularly.
notes:
The page's answer has two parts.

Plan for cloud-specific eviction as a separate step from on-prem remediation. The page says any on-prem compromise may have resulted in cloud-layer backdoors, so Azure AD, OAuth app permissions and federated identity providers must be independently reviewed and evicted.

Audit Azure AD federated identity providers, OAuth app permissions and service principals regularly, using Microsoft Entra audit logs or Defender for Cloud Apps to detect new high-privilege applications.

Bring the group's answers back to this. If a plan ends when the on-premises malware is gone, the page's guidance is that the cloud identity layer still needs its own review and eviction.

== What would you add for stages 02 and 03?
layout: question
No defense on the page names stage 02's DNS beaconing, or TEARDROP, BEACON and the domain admin escalation at stage 03.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty columns in the defense matrix.

At stage 02, SUNBURST beaconed over DNS, encoding victim environment information in subdomain queries, with traffic that mimicked legitimate SolarWinds telemetry. At stage 03, at selected high-value targets, TEARDROP deployed Cobalt Strike BEACON, and SVR obtained domain admin on the on-premises Active Directory, by a route the page says its sources do not state exactly.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 02 and 03
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own. Test it against reported details: DNS subdomain encoding, SUNBURST posing as the Orion Improvement Program, TEARDROP in memory, and BEACON's separate C2 domain.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 02 or stage 03, and this deck adds none of its own.

One page defense comes close. Treating ADFS as a Tier 0 asset protects the ADFS server, the goal of stage 03's on-premises access, though the theft it guards against happened at stage 04. The group may argue that it reaches stage 03; that argument is the group's own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports. For stage 02: the DNS subdomain queries to avsvmcloud[.]com that encoded victim environment information, and traffic that mimicked legitimate SolarWinds telemetry, masquerading as the Orion Improvement Program protocol, per FireEye. For stage 03: TEARDROP, which FireEye calls a memory-only dropper; Cobalt Strike BEACON, whose sample Unit 42 analyzed contains a beacon request to a separate domain, mobilnweb[.]com; and domain admin, reached by a route the sources do not state exactly.

